Vendored from https://github.com/Norbyte/lua-dos at commit
4fff4e19954f927a0478055306dadaf71bba02b2 (Lua 5.3.6 plus Norbyte's changes).

This is the same Lua the Baldur's Gate 3 Script Extender builds against, and
using it is not optional if bg3se's Lua binding layer is to be reused: that
layer depends on additions this fork makes to the interpreter itself, not just
on its own code. Specifically it needs the LUA_TCPPOBJECT value type, the
CMetatable hook in ltm.h, and LUA_EXTRASPACE widened to two pointers.

It also means mods see the Lua version bg3se mods are written against. bg3le
previously vendored stock Lua 5.4.9, which cannot host that layer.

Statically linked: an LD_PRELOAD shim loaded inside the Steam runtime
container cannot rely on host libraries being present. lua.c and luac.c (the
standalone interpreter and compiler mains) are omitted.

Lua is MIT licensed; see the copyright notice at the end of lua.h. The fork is
by Norbyte, to whom thanks are owed for this and for bg3se generally.

Local changes, both for bg3le's enum values (proxy userdata, not cppobjects,
since bg3le does not use the cppobject machinery for its own objects):

- lvm.c, luaV_equalobj: an enum value compares through its __eq with a label
  string or a number, as bg3se's cppobject enum values do.
- ltable.c, canonicalize_key: an enum value used as a table key stands for its
  label string, as bg3se's LuaCppCanonicalize makes them.
  luaH_bg3leenumlabel (declared in ltable.h) is what recognises one: a full
  userdata whose metatable holds __bg3leEnumLabel.

And one for mods written on Windows:

- luaconf.h, lua_pointer2str: pointers print as MSVC prints %p, sixteen
  uppercase hex digits with no "0x" ("table: 000055D1A2B3C4E0"). glibc adds
  "0x", which tonumber(s, 16) rejects; Trials Ascension seeds its RNG from
  tostring({}) that way and failed to load.
